cartridge "ARENA/AHOY!/1986"

' ARENA
' A ColecoVision game based on "Arena", Ahoy! February 1986 (Commodore 64
' machine language, ARENA at $C000, started by the two-line BASIC loader
' ARENA BOOT; the program and the disk do not name the author). Made for
' Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/arena.dis), the game is built for the ColecoVision:
' a stone-walled pit with torches, a spinning two-colour baton, the ten
' foe shapes re-cut as 16 x 16 sprites, bursts, sound effects and a tune.
'
' The game: you are a spinning baton in an arena with six foes that drift
' and bounce off the walls. The baton never stops: the stick turns it up,
' down, left or right, and it bounces off the walls by itself. Hold the
' button and push the stick to throw a shot that way (one shot at a time).
' Touch a live foe and you lose a life and the whole wave comes back. Clear
' all six for the next level: new foe shapes, a faster game, and each foe
' worth more (as many points as the level number). Three lives.
'
' From the original (orig/): the main loop's order (shot a pixel, hit
' test, shot a pixel, hit test, every foe a pixel, the baton a pixel, the
' stick); its delay loop, speed choice SLOW/MEDIUM/FAST (12/10/8 outer
' passes) and the 10 speed-ups (inner count 255 down to 155 by 10 a
' level), worked out into pixels per frame for the 60 Hz C64 (SpdT); a
' foe's direction on each axis is none, minus or plus by thirds of a random
' byte; a foe that meets a wall turns back on that axis and takes a random
' direction on the other; the baton turns back at a wall; the shot moves
' 2 pixels a pass and stops at the walls; a foe hit bursts for 25 passes
' and is gone; the wave restarts in full after a death; level N's foes are
' worth N points; ten foe shapes in turn; the start places (three foes in
' two rows on the left, the baton at the left wall, middle, heading
' right, every foe heading right); 3 lives; a high score; past 9999 the
' original said "I GIVE UP!!! - YOU WIN!!!".
'
' Liberties: the arena is 224 x 152 pixels (the C64's was 230 x 191);
' start places and speeds scaled to it (9/10 of the C64's pixels); box
' collision tests (the C64 used the sprite collision register); each
' burst has its own 25-pass clock (the C64 had one for all) and a burst
' cannot hurt the baton (on the C64 the debris still could); the button
' alone throws a shot the way the baton is heading (on the C64 it needed
' a direction); the score, level, lives and best on a status line at the
' top (the C64 had a column on the right); the C64's speed question is the
' title menu; GAME OVER (and the win message) then the title (the C64
' waited for fire or asked to quit).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick turns the baton; the button
' (either) throws a shot, aimed with the stick. On the title, up and down
' choose a speed or the credits, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers.
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs jake-return-before-for removed 2026-11-01.

const TFl0 = 128
const TFl1 = 129
